---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
---

<Base
	title="Loan payoff | Actual Budget Tweaks"
	description="See when a loan or line of credit is paid off and what it costs, in Actual Budget Tweaks."
>
	<main class="docs center">
		<header class="docs__header stack stack--sm">
			<a href="/guides" class="docs__back">Back to guides</a>
			<h1>Loan payoff</h1>
			<p class="docs__lead">
				Loan payoff works out when a loan, mortgage or line of credit is paid off and how much
				interest it costs on the way. Actual keeps the balance; you tell the panel the rate and
				the payment.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Setting up a loan</h2>
			<p>Turn on Loan payoff in the Features section of Tweaks, then:</p>
			<ol>
				<li>Open an account that owes money and click <strong>Payoff</strong> in its toolbar.</li>
				<li>Enter the annual rate and your monthly payment.</li>
				<li>
					Pick how interest is charged: <strong>Daily</strong> on each day's balance, as credit
					cards and lines of credit do, or <strong>Monthly</strong> as a twelfth of the yearly
					rate, as mortgages and car loans do.
				</li>
				<li>Enter the days of the month the interest posts and the payment lands.</li>
				<li>Click <strong>Save and see payoff</strong>.</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				Once saved, the account shows when it will be paid off beside its balance, and the details
				fold away into <strong>Loan settings</strong>, where you can change or remove them. Both
				the panel and the figure beside the balance follow the account as transactions are added.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Reading the plan</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Paid off by</strong> is the month of the last payment, with how long that is from
					now and the interest still to come.
				</li>
				<li>
					The bar shows how far the debt has come down from the most it's been since the account
					last owed nothing. On a line of credit you borrow from again, it starts over from there.
				</li>
				<li>
					The chart shows what you owed over the past year and what you'll owe each month until it
					reaches zero.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Next payment</strong> splits your next payment into what goes to the principal
					and what goes to interest.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Trying out changes</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>Try a change</strong> runs the saved plan with something different, and shows
				beside the payoff month how much sooner it finishes and what it does to the interest:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Payment</strong> slides the monthly payment up or down. Type in the box beside it
					for an exact amount.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Finish by</strong> takes a month and sets the payment that clears the debt by
					then.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Extra payments</strong> adds one-off payments. They are for trying out and
					aren't saved.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Nothing changes the saved plan until you click <strong>Use this plan</strong>, which saves
				the payment you tried. <strong>Reset</strong> goes back to the plan.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Interest that hasn't been posted</h2>
			<p>
				Enter the payee your lender's interest charges are posted under as the
				<strong>Interest payee</strong> in Loan settings. The panel then knows when interest was
				last charged, and works out the next charge from the balance on each day since.
			</p>
			<p>
				If an interest day has passed and the charge isn't in the account, it's listed under the
				chart, and <strong>Add</strong> enters it as an uncleared transaction, which you can undo
				from the notification.
			</p>
			<p>
				The rate isn't checked against what the lender charges, so update it in Loan settings when
				a variable rate changes.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Where the settings are kept</h2>
			<p>
				Each loan's details are kept with Tweaks' other settings for the budget. They sync with
				the budget, so they follow you to another device with Tweaks installed, and they are part
				of a settings backup. <strong>Remove loan details</strong> in Loan settings forgets them
				for that account.
			</p>
			<p>
				Nothing is written to the account itself, apart from an interest transaction when you
				click <strong>Add</strong>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>What it assumes</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>One rate and one payment from today onward, and no new borrowing.</li>
				<li>The payment is the same every month, not a share of the balance.</li>
				<li>Lenders round and count days in their own ways, so expect small differences.</li>
			</ul>
		</section>
	</main>
</Base>
